.TH "lfe_modules" "7" "2026" ""
.SH MODULES
.SS Module Syntax
LFE code is divided into modules.
A module consists of a sequence of attributes and function declarations,
each being a valid form.
.SS Module Attributes
A \f[I]module attribute\f[R] defines a certain property of a module.
A module attribute consists of a tag and a value.
The \f[CR]tag\f[R] is an atom and the \f[CR]value\f[R] must be a literal
term.
.PP
Several module attributes have predefined meanings.
Some of them have arity two, but user\-defined module attributes must
have arity one.
.SS Pre\-Defined Module Attributes
\f[B]\f[CB](module module\-name)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Module declaration, defining the name of the module.
The name \f[CR]module\-name\f[R], an atom, is to be same as the file
name minus the extension \f[CR].lfe\f[R].
Otherwise code loading does not work as intended.
.PP
This attribute is to be specified first and is the only mandatory
attribute.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](export functions)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Exported functions.
Specifies which of the functions, defined within the module, that are
visible from outside the module.
.PP
\f[CR]functions\f[R] is a list
\f[CR]((name\-1 arity\-1) ... (name\-n arity\-n))\f[R], where each
\f[CR]name\-i\f[R] is an atom and \f[CR]arity\-i\f[R] an integer.
It can also be just the atom \f[CR]all\f[R] which means that all
functions are exported.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](import module functions)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Importing allows us to call \(lqlocal\(rq functions where the call will
be changed to a call to the function in another module, so the call
\f[CR](do\-stuff x y)\f[R] will become the call
\f[CR](the\-module:do\-stuff x y)\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]functions\f[R] is a list \f[CR](name\-1 name\-2 ... name\-n)\f[R]
of the functions which will be changed to a call to the given module.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](rename module renames)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Like \f[CR]import\f[R] but it allows us to rename the function being
called, so in this case the call \f[CR](funny\-name x y)\f[R] could
become \f[CR](the\-module:good\-name x y)\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](moduledoc documentation)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The user documentation for this module.
The allowed values for \f[CR]documentation\f[R] are the same as for
\f[CR]doc\f[R] attribute.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](compile options)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Compiler options.
\f[CR]options\f[R] is a single option or a list of options.
This attribute is added to the option list when compiling the module.
See module compile in Compiler.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](vsn vsn)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Module version.
\f[CR]vsn\f[R] is any literal term and can be retrieved using
\f[CR]beam_lib:version/1\f[R].
.PP
If this attribute is not specified, the version defaults to the MD5
checksum of the module.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](on_load functions)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
This attribute names a function that is to be run automatically when a
module is loaded.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](nifs functions)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Specifies which of the functions, defined within the module, that may be
loaded as NIFs with \f[CR]erlang:load_nif/2\f[R].
.SS Behaviour Module Attribute
It is possible to specify that the module is the callback module for a
\f[I]behaviour\f[R]:
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](behaviour behaviour)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
The atom \f[CR]behaviour\f[R] gives the name of the behaviour, which can
be a user\-defined behaviour or one of the following OTP standard
behaviours:
.IP
.EX
gen_server
gen_statem
gen_event
supervisor
.EE
.PP
The spelling \f[CR]behavior\f[R] is also accepted.
.SS Record and Struct definitions
The same syntax as for module attributes is used for record and struct
definitions:
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](record name fields)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](struct fields)\f[B]\f[R]
.SS Setting File and Line
The same syntax as for module attributes is used for changing the
pre\-defined macros \f[CR]FILE\f[R] and \f[CR]LINE\f[R]:
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](file name line)\f[B]\f[R]
.SS Types and function specifications
A similar syntax as for module attributes is used for specifying types
and function specifications:
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](type type\-name type\-def)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](opaque type\-name type\-def)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](spec function function\-spec)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
For a description of these see the \f[CR]lfe\-types\f[R] documentation.
.SS Documentation attributes
The module attribute \f[CR](doc documentation)\f[R] is used to provide
user documentation for a function/type/callback.
.IP
.EX
(doc \(dqExample documentation\(dq)
(function example (lambda () \(aqok))
.EE
.PP
The attribute should be placed just before the entity it documents.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](doc documentation)\f[B]\f[R]
.SS LFE specific attributes
\f[B]\f[CB](alias module\-name alias)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Provide alias names for modules.
The \f[CR]alias\f[R] can be used in code as an alternative to
\f[CR]module\-name\f[R].
A typical use is to provide short names for modules with long names.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](module\-alias aliases)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Provide alias names for modules.
The alias can be used in code as an alternative to modulename.
A typical use is to provide short names for modules with long names.
\f[CR]aliases\f[R] is a list:
.PP
\f[CR]((real\-module\-name\-1 alias\-1) ... (real\-module\-name\-n alias\-n))\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](export\-macro macros)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Export the macros so they can be callable from other modules.
.PP
\f[CR]macros\f[R] is a list \f[CR](name\-1 ... name\-n)\f[R], where each
\f[CR]name\-i\f[R] is an atom.
It can also be just the atom \f[CR]all\f[R] which means that all macros
are exported.
.SS User\-defined attributes
User\-defined module attributes must have arity one.
There are two ways of creating user\-defined attributes, either using
the more literal \f[CR]attribute\f[R] tag or going more Erlangy with
\f[CR]\-\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](attribute tag value)\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](\- tag value)\f[B]\f[R]
.SS Comments
Comments come in two forms: line comments and block comments.
.PP
Line comments start with a semicolon \f[CR];\f[R] and finish with the
end of the line.
.PP
Block comments are written as \f[CR]#| comment text |#\f[R] where the
comment text may span multiple lines but my not contain another block
comment, i.e.\ it may not contain the character sequence \f[CR]#|\f[R].
.SS Older Forms
.IP
.EX
(defmodule module\-name
    attributes)
.EE
